  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== SECTION 1: OVERVIEW ===== -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
  <p>
    This report examines every winner of the Academy Award for Best Original Score from the 6th Academy Awards
    ceremony in 1934 through the 98th ceremony in March 2026. The dataset comprises <strong>93 rows</strong> across
    3 columns — <em>Year</em>, <em>Film</em>, and <em>Composer</em> — drawn from the Official Academy Awards Database
    and cross-referenced with IMDb and MusicBrainz. Each row represents one ceremony year and its corresponding
    winning film and composer(s).
  </p>
  <p>
    The Best Original Score category has undergone multiple name changes and occasional splits (e.g., separate
    awards for dramatic and comedy/musical scores in certain years), but this dataset consolidates to one winner
    per year. Composer names have been standardized and normalized; where multiple composers shared the award,
    they appear as individual entries. After decomposition, the data yields <strong>79 distinct composers</strong>
    honored across <strong>93 unique winning films</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-pill">Years Span: <strong>1934–2026</strong></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Total Ceremonies: <strong>93</strong></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Unique Films: <strong>93</strong></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Unique Composers: <strong>79</strong></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Repeat Winners: <strong>14 composers</strong></div>
    <div class="stat-pill">Single-Win Composers: <strong>65</strong></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    At 28.0%, just over a quarter of all ceremonies were won by composers who had already claimed the prize at
    least once before — a figure that speaks to both the breadth of talent recognized by the Academy and the
    difficulty of sustained dominance in a field where the output cycle (one score per film) naturally limits
    repeat opportunities.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 2: TOP COMPOSERS ===== -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <h2>2. Most Awarded Composers</h2>
  <p>
    Three composers share the record for most Best Original Score Oscars, each with <strong>4 wins</strong>:
    John Barry, John Williams, and Ludwig Göransson. This tie at the top is remarkable for spanning three
    distinct eras — Barry's wins cluster in the Silver and Modern eras (1966–1990), Williams' across the
    Silver and Modern eras (1975–1993), and Göransson's entirely within the Contemporary era (2018–2026),
    marking him as the most rapidly ascending figure in Oscar scoring history.
  </p>

