    26 ceremonies, this era was remarkably un-concentrated — only Max Steiner (3 wins) and Dimitri Tiomkin (3)
    managed repeat victories. The films themselves were predominantly historical epics, literary adaptations, and
    melodramas: <em>The Adventures of Robin Hood</em>, <em>Ben-Hur</em>, <em>The Wizard of Oz</em>. Composers
    like Steiner, Alfred Newman, Miklos Rozsa, and Franz Waxman — many of them European émigrés — defined a
    lush, symphonic "Hollywood sound" rooted in late-Romantic orchestral traditions.
  </p>

  <h3>Silver Age (1960–1979): Experimentation and the Blockbuster Dawn</h3>
  <p>
    This era saw the highest first-time winner percentage (85%) and the arrival of several transformative voices.
    John Barry brought British sophistication (<em>Born Free</em>, <em>The Lion in Winter</em>); Maurice Jarre
    delivered sweeping epics (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>, <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>); and John Williams began his
    ascent with <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and the iconic <em>Star Wars</em> (1977). The 1970s in particular marked
    a turning point: Nino Rota's <em>Godfather</em> scores and Giorgio Moroder's electronic <em>Midnight Express</em>
    (1978) signaled a broadening of what an Oscar-winning score could sound like.
  </p>

  <h3>Modern Era (1980–1999): The Disney Renaissance and Synthesizers</h3>
  <p>
    Alan Menken's near-consecutive three-peat (1989, 1991, 1992) with the Disney animated musical renaissance
    — <em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em> — is the densest win
    cluster in Oscar history. This was also the era when John Williams added his third and fourth Oscars
    (<em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler's List</em>), and John Barry captured his third and fourth. The 1990s
    introduced Hans Zimmer (<em>The Lion King</em>, 1994), James Horner (<em>Titanic</em>, 1997), and
    a growing appetite for scores that blended orchestral traditions with electronic textures and world
    music influences.
  </p>

  <h3>Contemporary Era (2000–2026): Global Voices and Genre Fluidity</h3>
  <p>
    The Contemporary era is defined by unprecedented stylistic range and international reach. The roster
    of winners spans Chinese composer Tan Dun (<em>Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon</em>), Indian composer
    A.R. Rahman (<em>Slumdog Millionaire</em>), Icelandic composer Hildur Guðnadóttir (<em>Joker</em>),
    and German composer Volker Bertelmann (<em>All Quiet on the Western Front</em>). Trent Reznor and
    Atticus Ross brought industrial and electronic textures from Nine Inch Nails into the Oscar fold
    (<em>The Social Network</em>, <em>Soul</em>). And Ludwig Göransson has emerged as the era's defining
    figure, with four wins in eight years — a pace unmatched by any composer in history — spanning
    <em>Black Panther</em> (2018), <em>Oppenheimer</em> (2023–2024), and <em>Sinners</em> (2026).
  </p>
